WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.757
I find myself following the advice
that it is better to ask for

00:00:04.757 --> 00:00:08.520
forgiveness than ask for permission.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.840
The end result.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.180
I am often apologizing.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:17.740
Today, we will explore apologies,
forgiveness and happiness.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.921
I'm Matt Abraham and

00:00:18.921 --> 00:00:23.570
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:27.150
Welcome to Think Fast,
Talk Smart, the podcast.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:32.681
I am excited and really interested to
speak with Dr. Fred Luskin, who is

00:00:32.681 --> 00:00:37.710
the director of the Stanford Forgiveness
Project and the author of two books.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:41.840
Forgive for good, A Proven Prescription
for Health and Happiness and

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.680
Stress Free for Good.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.480
10 proven life skills for
health and happiness.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.020
Welcome to the podcast Fred,
thanks for joining me.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.635
&gt;&gt; Thanks for having me on.

00:00:51.635 --> 00:00:53.335
&gt;&gt; Let's get started.

00:00:53.335 --> 00:00:57.005
From your perspective,
why is forgiving important?

00:00:57.005 --> 00:01:01.955
And can you share your view on the
forgiving process and the steps involved?

00:01:03.962 --> 00:01:07.542
&gt;&gt; Desmond Tutu says without forgiveness,
there is no future.

00:01:08.832 --> 00:01:14.851
So implying that if you don't let go
of the past to release your bitterness,

00:01:14.851 --> 00:01:20.582
you living in the past, that is
really dangerous for relationships.

00:01:20.582 --> 00:01:25.010
And in terms of I know what
you teach is communication.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:28.730
You don't want to be just negotiating and
relitigating the past.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:34.232
You want to have communication now,
about what's going on now.

00:01:34.232 --> 00:01:38.808
We through the Forgiveness Project
have developed a nine step

00:01:38.808 --> 00:01:43.299
process to forgiveness and
that's the essence of our work.

00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:47.685
It goes from awarenesses of
what forgiveness is and isn't,

00:01:47.685 --> 00:01:49.480
to some practices.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:54.650
The end result being you end up
with an entirely different story,

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:58.228
that you tell yourself
about what happened.

00:01:58.228 --> 00:02:03.646
&gt;&gt; Fascinating, how does apologizing
fit into this forgiving process and

00:02:03.646 --> 00:02:07.658
what are the steps that
are involved in apologizing?

00:02:07.658 --> 00:02:10.286
I find myself doing it quite often.

00:02:10.286 --> 00:02:13.619
&gt;&gt; Well in particular if you
don't want to ask for permission.

00:02:13.619 --> 00:02:15.892
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; You're going to have a good number of

00:02:15.892 --> 00:02:17.080
apologies.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:21.978
Apology is one of the few things that
research shows actually facilitates

00:02:21.978 --> 00:02:24.272
forgiveness and is not that many.

00:02:24.272 --> 00:02:27.400
A sincere apology is a facilitator.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.281
A sincere apology is what's important and

00:02:31.281 --> 00:02:36.454
from what I understand most
apologies are not that useful

00:02:36.454 --> 00:02:41.750
because they end up saying,
I'm sorry that you feel bad.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:46.860
Or I'm sorry that you're upset.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.600
A sincere apology says I did wrong.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.680
My wrong lead to your upset.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:01.190
I own both my own behavior and
your response.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:02.896
I'm sorry about that.

00:03:02.896 --> 00:03:06.267
And if I can, I will make amends.

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:09.240
So that's the fullness of apology.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:14.140
We add to that in terms of
the forgiveness peace is not only

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:17.202
would it be nice to make amends, but

00:03:17.202 --> 00:03:22.340
we good if I can undergo
whatever processes are needed.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.922
So I'm less likely to do it again.

00:03:24.922 --> 00:03:31.640
So if I do something because I have a bad
habit, do something to work on that habit.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.374
If I'm a drunk, stop drinking.

00:03:34.374 --> 00:03:38.655
If I have a bad temper,
acknowledge and get some help.

00:03:38.655 --> 00:03:42.121
But the apology is that I see your hurt,

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:46.638
I own my own behavior that
caused that I'm sorry and

00:03:46.638 --> 00:03:50.539
is there anything I can
do to make it better.

00:03:50.539 --> 00:03:54.483
&gt;&gt; So it starts with taking
responsibility and includes empathy.

00:03:54.483 --> 00:04:00.753
&gt;&gt; And that's why it's so hard that many
people struggle to admit their flaws and

00:04:00.753 --> 00:04:06.551
own the fact that behavior of theirs
caused other people's suffering.

00:04:06.551 --> 00:04:12.233
Or their own sufferings such as,
I did something really stupid and

00:04:12.233 --> 00:04:15.490
blew off the test or something, yes.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:19.963
&gt;&gt; Interesting, so those people who listen
into the podcast know I'm a huge fan of

00:04:19.963 --> 00:04:23.257
structure and
how you can structure your communication.

00:04:23.257 --> 00:04:26.338
And it sounds like
a recommendation you have as

00:04:26.338 --> 00:04:30.762
you start by acknowledging your
responsibility and what you did.

00:04:30.762 --> 00:04:36.351
And then move to talk about how what you
did impacted to the other or others,

00:04:36.351 --> 00:04:41.422
and from there can talk about how
you can go about making amends and.

00:04:41.422 --> 00:04:43.186
&gt;&gt; That would be a good thing.

00:04:43.186 --> 00:04:47.814
I'm going to say even more deeply though,
I would advocate for

00:04:47.814 --> 00:04:49.910
the value of mindfulness.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:56.074
Or even a Buddhist base awareness practice
that it's really good to pay attention

00:04:56.074 --> 00:05:02.130
to both our behavior, our thoughts,
and how they influence other people.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:06.820
You don't want it to be limited
just to when you cause harm.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:10.894
Because in a deeper way,
the more important communication or

00:05:10.894 --> 00:05:14.355
the positive or
facilitative communication, so.

00:05:14.355 --> 00:05:18.435
To say to somebody I noticed
what you did that was good, or

00:05:18.435 --> 00:05:22.680
I noticed that I was
feeling love towards you.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:28.094
Those are mindfulness space
communications that are really helpful for

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:33.700
the relationship that apology and
the forgiveness or repair parts.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:36.237
There will also facilitative parts.

00:05:36.237 --> 00:05:40.090
&gt;&gt; Wow, I almost think of the Yin Yang
where you need that shit together to have

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:42.242
the whole relationship with the person.

00:05:42.242 --> 00:05:44.981
&gt;&gt; And
the research on relationship success.

00:05:44.981 --> 00:05:52.120
They used to think that it was mostly
that people could fight in a certain way.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:55.687
But now they're finding that
a large part of relationship

00:05:55.687 --> 00:06:00.146
access is does your partner or
your friend acknowledge your successes.

00:06:00.146 --> 00:06:04.652
And the more you feel like you can
share your positive world with

00:06:04.652 --> 00:06:07.981
somebody the better
the relationship feels.

00:06:07.981 --> 00:06:12.222
&gt;&gt; I had read that and you just reminded
me of that, that it's the celebrating of

00:06:12.222 --> 00:06:15.760
the successes Jagan little that
is also critically important.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.700
&gt;&gt; Critically important.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:19.799
&gt;&gt; Wonderful, thank you for that.

00:06:19.799 --> 00:06:23.885
On this podcast Fred we have often
talked about how our mindset and

00:06:23.885 --> 00:06:29.400
approach affects our communication, but
we've not explored our internal self talk.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.980
Something you've thought a lot about and
I'm wondering can you help us understand

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:38.114
the importance of self talk and how it
affects how we interact with others.

00:06:38.114 --> 00:06:42.658
&gt;&gt; I mean,
we're always evaluating experiences.

00:06:42.658 --> 00:06:46.901
So, I might have good question to you.

00:06:46.901 --> 00:06:48.925
Good answer to me.

00:06:48.925 --> 00:06:50.750
Not good question from you.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:53.700
Not good question, answer from me.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:07:00.210
We have an almost relentless inner
dialogue that evaluates things.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:08.408
If we just allow that critic praiser
to go, we're pulled all over the place.

00:07:08.408 --> 00:07:11.843
So, good question, all right,
I feel better bad question.

00:07:11.843 --> 00:07:13.646
I feel worse, good answer.

00:07:13.646 --> 00:07:15.080
I feel good, bad question.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:18.390
I feel bad,
it's an emotional roller coaster.

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:28.000
So when people have meta strategies above
that, that can modulate the self talk.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.486
They usually have more control over it and
their emotion so.

00:07:31.486 --> 00:07:34.103
A mindset is a word for that.

00:07:34.103 --> 00:07:36.557
So, I have a growth mindset.

00:07:36.557 --> 00:07:40.424
So I want to learn how to have
more constructive self talk.

00:07:40.424 --> 00:07:41.058
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:07:41.058 --> 00:07:43.951
&gt;&gt; The fixed mindset,
that's just who I am.

00:07:43.951 --> 00:07:47.055
&gt;&gt; All right.
&gt;&gt; I teach happiness mindsets.

00:07:47.055 --> 00:07:47.895
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:07:47.895 --> 00:07:52.287
&gt;&gt; So, what kind of self
talk will make me happier?

00:07:52.287 --> 00:07:56.444
But if you have the happiness
mindset then you look at

00:07:56.444 --> 00:07:59.376
yourself talk from that perspective

00:07:59.376 --> 00:08:02.338
is it working towards my deeper goal
&gt;&gt; So

00:08:02.338 --> 00:08:04.348
you can actually do work on your mindset.

00:08:04.348 --> 00:08:08.707
Which then influences and affects how
you approach your communication and

00:08:08.707 --> 00:08:12.247
the different ways in which you
interact with other people.

00:08:12.247 --> 00:08:17.066
&gt;&gt; I think it's a bidirectional
communication that when you wake up in

00:08:17.066 --> 00:08:21.473
the morning, we have a handful
of very basic inner premises.

00:08:21.473 --> 00:08:22.109
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:08:22.109 --> 00:08:27.380
&gt;&gt; And that reflects in that self talk and
how we use the self talk.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:30.992
But we can also look at the self talk and
pull back and

00:08:30.992 --> 00:08:35.111
recognize what our basic,
deepest goals and values are.

00:08:35.111 --> 00:08:35.733
So it's.

00:08:35.733 --> 00:08:36.460
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:38.518
&gt;&gt; So bidirectional communication.

00:08:38.518 --> 00:08:41.499
&gt;&gt; I've explored this a little
bit when I help people manage

00:08:41.499 --> 00:08:43.745
anxiety they have around communication.

00:08:43.745 --> 00:08:44.339
&gt;&gt; Of course.

00:08:44.339 --> 00:08:47.226
&gt;&gt; And we have lots of negative self
talk that we say to ourselves I'm

00:08:47.226 --> 00:08:49.653
going to mess up I can't
believe I'm in this situation.

00:08:49.653 --> 00:08:50.820
&gt;&gt; I'm a failure.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:55.575
&gt;&gt; Right and if you start understanding
what you're saying to yourself,

00:08:55.575 --> 00:08:58.517
you can then begin to
change that self talk.

00:08:58.517 --> 00:08:59.682
&gt;&gt; Exactly.
&gt;&gt; And there are words that

00:08:59.682 --> 00:09:02.190
mindset that can help you feel better and
more confident.

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:05.330
So I see a lot of synergy between what
you're saying and some of the work I do.

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:09.243
I want to tap into this notion of
happiness because your focus on

00:09:09.243 --> 00:09:13.319
forgiveness really is part of
your larger work gone happiness.

00:09:13.319 --> 00:09:17.660
Can you help us connect the dots between
forgiveness, apology, mindset and

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:18.410
happiness?

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:19.920
How do those all connect together?

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.940
And are there a couple of things
we could do to be more happy?

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:28.504
&gt;&gt; There was a book maybe 15,
20 years ago that the Dalai Lama wrote.

00:09:28.504 --> 00:09:29.163
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:09:29.163 --> 00:09:30.527
&gt;&gt; Called The Art of Happiness.

00:09:30.527 --> 00:09:31.561
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:09:31.561 --> 00:09:36.670
&gt;&gt; And in that book the thing he talked
about that was most influential to

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:42.502
me was if you want to be happy, practice
the conditions that lead to happiness.

00:09:42.502 --> 00:09:43.137
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:09:43.137 --> 00:09:47.410
&gt;&gt; If you want to be unhappy, practice
the conditions that lead to unhappiness.

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:51.935
So, within that framework of strategies
that are more likely to produce

00:09:51.935 --> 00:09:56.590
happiness and those more likely
to produce that happiness.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:10:00.247
One of the strongest that produces
unhappiness is grievance,

00:10:00.247 --> 00:10:04.560
is walking around with an unresolved
grudge against something.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:08.950
Another one that leads to unhappiness
is bitterness that people feel

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:10.999
life hasn't been fair to them.

00:10:10.999 --> 00:10:14.416
And a third that we see all
the time is just self pity,

00:10:14.416 --> 00:10:17.378
the poor me it didn't
work out the way I hope.

00:10:17.378 --> 00:10:22.644
Those are practices that the Dalai Lama
says lead to unhappiness.

00:10:22.644 --> 00:10:26.379
Practices that we teach that
lead more to happiness,

00:10:26.379 --> 00:10:32.366
forgiveness is resolving those grudges,
gratitude is the opposite of bitterness.

00:10:32.366 --> 00:10:33.073
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:10:33.073 --> 00:10:38.047
&gt;&gt; And a certain self affirmation
would be a contrary practice that

00:10:38.047 --> 00:10:40.452
would lead to more happiness.

00:10:40.452 --> 00:10:43.700
The key definer of that is again,

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:48.224
what we practice influences
how happy we are.

00:10:48.224 --> 00:10:52.638
It's not based primarily
in external experiences,

00:10:52.638 --> 00:10:56.766
it's an inner way of
holding those experiences.

00:10:56.766 --> 00:11:00.546
&gt;&gt; The cataloguing of the things we
do to make ourselves unhappy and

00:11:00.546 --> 00:11:04.756
make happy is actually very profound
I find myself really reflecting.

00:11:04.756 --> 00:11:08.410
I find that one of the things that makes
me unhappy as I'm over scheduled and

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:09.288
rushing around.

00:11:09.288 --> 00:11:09.862
&gt;&gt; Of course.

00:11:09.862 --> 00:11:13.382
&gt;&gt; And so giving ourselves time and space
and prior to us starting the recording,

00:11:13.382 --> 00:11:16.600
you shared that meditation is
something you do, it's something I do.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:21.428
And it strikes me that just taking time
to reflect, to give yourself that space,

00:11:21.428 --> 00:11:22.777
in your schedule, and

00:11:22.777 --> 00:11:27.039
maybe even to meditate are ways to
help allow us to explore these ideas.

00:11:27.039 --> 00:11:27.667
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:11:27.667 --> 00:11:29.680
&gt;&gt; But also to make ourselves happier.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.443
&gt;&gt; I mean, meditation is a profound tool.

00:11:34.443 --> 00:11:36.239
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:11:36.239 --> 00:11:39.664
&gt;&gt; That some people think
is another necessary type

00:11:39.664 --> 00:11:42.260
of consciousness that we minimize.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:45.871
So, we have waking, sleeping, dreaming.

00:11:45.871 --> 00:11:48.444
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; There are people who postulate that

00:11:48.444 --> 00:11:52.230
meditation and
it's in on its self a brain patterning.

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:57.010
That is required for
our highest functioning.

00:11:57.010 --> 00:12:02.169
&gt;&gt; Fred, you've been teaching these skills
for a long time and I'm curious if you

00:12:02.169 --> 00:12:07.187
have any best practices you rely on to
help people learn these types of tools.

00:12:07.187 --> 00:12:09.353
So when you teach these tools,

00:12:09.353 --> 00:12:14.284
what are things you have found help
you to educate people about these.

00:12:14.284 --> 00:12:18.504
&gt;&gt; I'm going to say the tool
that would be most essential for

00:12:18.504 --> 00:12:22.183
my simple practices
are abdominal breathing.

00:12:22.183 --> 00:12:22.865
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:12:22.865 --> 00:12:27.553
Where you really focus on your core
like somewhere in the the centre

00:12:27.553 --> 00:12:31.331
like the martial arts put it
an inch below your navel.

00:12:31.331 --> 00:12:32.103
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:12:32.103 --> 00:12:35.692
&gt;&gt; But inhaling and
exhaling from that point and

00:12:35.692 --> 00:12:40.893
making sure the exhalation is deep so
the next inhalation fills.

00:12:40.893 --> 00:12:46.061
That's essential and the other
essential practice is simply thank you.

00:12:46.061 --> 00:12:49.470
So I'm going to say the other
practices to train your mind so

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:53.162
that you find all the ways that
people are kind in good to you and

00:12:53.162 --> 00:12:56.160
all the ways that you're
supported in the world.

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:02.140
And that results in a heck of a lot more
thank you than most people end up having.

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:06.100
But just noticing, This a water fountain.

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:09.060
&gt;&gt; Wow, that's good I don't
have to go dig up water.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:09.759
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:13:09.759 --> 00:13:13.478
&gt;&gt; And if you create a small
attitude of thank you from that,

00:13:13.478 --> 00:13:15.890
that in and of itself lowers stress.

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:19.780
But again, it opens the perception
as the world is a safe place.

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:26.416
And that probably is the most important
thing that we can create for ourselves.

00:13:26.416 --> 00:13:27.677
&gt;&gt; An attitude of gratitude.

00:13:27.677 --> 00:13:29.337
[LAUGH] you got it.

00:13:29.337 --> 00:13:32.914
&gt;&gt; Is a great way and we've definitely
talked about the importance of breath and

00:13:32.914 --> 00:13:34.462
communication and relaxation.

00:13:34.462 --> 00:13:35.205
&gt;&gt; Of course.
&gt;&gt; And

00:13:35.205 --> 00:13:38.666
it's great to hear it
echoed in your ideas.

00:13:38.666 --> 00:13:39.511
Before we end,

00:13:39.511 --> 00:13:43.672
I'd like to ask you the same three
questions I asked everybody who joins me.

00:13:43.672 --> 00:13:45.041
Yep for that?

00:13:45.041 --> 00:13:46.370
&gt;&gt; Sure
&gt;&gt; All right,

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:51.435
if you were to capture the best
communication advice you have ever

00:13:51.435 --> 00:13:56.830
received as a five to seven word,
presentation slide title.

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:57.830
What would that be?

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:03.349
&gt;&gt; Try as best you can for kind speech.

00:14:03.349 --> 00:14:05.860
&gt;&gt; Try as best you can for
kind speech, I like that.

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:08.570
It's not directive, it's inviting.

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:10.526
&gt;&gt; And it's also realistic.

00:14:10.526 --> 00:14:14.506
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and don't set expectations
that are unachievable.

00:14:14.506 --> 00:14:15.269
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:14:15.269 --> 00:14:18.215
&gt;&gt; A lot of us set expectations for
ourselves and invite.

00:14:18.215 --> 00:14:19.899
&gt;&gt; Other people absolutely.

00:14:19.899 --> 00:14:25.300
&gt;&gt; I like that it's, in some ways it's
forgiving yourself before you even start.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:27.254
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK].

00:14:27.254 --> 00:14:33.336
Let me ask you a question number two, who
is a communicator that you admire and why?

00:14:33.336 --> 00:14:38.736
&gt;&gt; I had a teacher who when I
was getting my first graduate

00:14:38.736 --> 00:14:44.018
degree at San Jose State,
I watched him do therapy.

00:14:44.018 --> 00:14:46.775
And his listening was so profound.

00:14:46.775 --> 00:14:47.439
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:14:47.439 --> 00:14:53.512
&gt;&gt; His attention was so
focused on someone else.

00:14:53.512 --> 00:14:57.419
That struck me as how deeply
valuable that was for

00:14:57.419 --> 00:15:03.720
any kind of communication practice because
he was all there in his listening.

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:04.266
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:15:04.266 --> 00:15:09.133
&gt;&gt; And that observing behavior
taught me something that was really

00:15:09.133 --> 00:15:11.755
profoundly helpful in my career.

00:15:12.945 --> 00:15:15.557
&gt;&gt; I love that I asked you
a question about a communicator and

00:15:15.557 --> 00:15:17.995
you immediately defaulted to a listener.

00:15:17.995 --> 00:15:21.978
And I think that is so important in our
communication to remember that listening

00:15:21.978 --> 00:15:25.435
is as important if not more important,
when we're communicating.

00:15:26.775 --> 00:15:28.400
&gt;&gt; Question number three.

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:34.108
What are the first three ingredients
that go into a successful

00:15:34.108 --> 00:15:39.270
communication recipe that
you're communicating when

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:45.106
somebody who is similar to you
in deep values and aspirations?

00:15:45.106 --> 00:15:50.151
That it's your responsibility
to be clear and understandable.

00:15:50.151 --> 00:15:53.243
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And you have opportunities if you don't

00:15:53.243 --> 00:15:58.650
do it right the first time, if you're
humble to get more attempts at it.

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:04.540
&gt;&gt; So I like that you're seeking
similarity in terms of attitudes,

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:06.000
beliefs and values.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:06.630
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:10.955
&gt;&gt; And I love that last part about,
be humble and try it again.

00:16:10.955 --> 00:16:14.767
&gt;&gt; One of the practices that we do
teaching forgiveness is some aspect of

00:16:14.767 --> 00:16:16.948
Buddha's loving kindness practice.

00:16:16.948 --> 00:16:18.323
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:16:18.323 --> 00:16:25.167
&gt;&gt; So one of those practices is basically
just like me, they want to be happy.

00:16:25.167 --> 00:16:29.823
Just like me, they want to be a piece,
just like me, whatever.

00:16:29.823 --> 00:16:35.559
And I think if the communication matters,
you'd want to have that somewhere

00:16:35.559 --> 00:16:40.770
in your consciousness that just like me,
they want similar things.

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:42.290
&gt;&gt; So seeking commonality.

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:42.971
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:16:42.971 --> 00:16:44.018
&gt;&gt; And similarity is a way to connect.

00:16:44.018 --> 00:16:46.349
If I had to choose one.

00:16:46.349 --> 00:16:49.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I like that.

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:52.796
Well, Fred I certainly have to
end this by saying, thank you.

00:16:52.796 --> 00:16:54.801
&gt;&gt; You welcome.
&gt;&gt; You taught me a lot about gratitude,

00:16:54.801 --> 00:16:56.970
your forgiveness and apology.

00:16:56.970 --> 00:17:01.900
I find your work fascinating and
so needed in our world today.

00:17:01.900 --> 00:17:03.873
Thank you for taking the time and

00:17:03.873 --> 00:17:09.060
helping us improve not just our
communication but perhaps our happiness.

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:09.560
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

